() Tj T*
(Risk for a record equals its calibrated logistic annual-label probability times the) Tj T*
(source number of users. The total is a customer-interruption proxy, with no assumed) Tj T*
(outage duration or monetary loss. Users enter as supplied counts, not as a derived lost-) Tj T*
(load estimate. This excludes safety, environmental and restoration consequences. Source) Tj T*
(scores: outputs/scores-2020.csv; interval scope: outputs/provenance.json.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The scenario assumes refurbishment costs CAD 6,000 and replacement CAD 12,000. Assumed) Tj T*
(reductions are 60% and 90%. These are labelled assumptions, not public price evidence or) Tj T*
(empirically identified treatment effects. They were fixed before scoring in) Tj T*
(config/study.json. All budget points are independent one-year scenarios; there is no) Tj T*
(forecast of deterioration, recurring benefit, discounting or multi-year asset) Tj T*
(transition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For these equal-unit costs and diminishing benefits, refurbishment supplies a first) Tj T*
(increment of benefit and replacement supplies an additional smaller increment at the) Tj T*
(same marginal cost. Sorting all marginal increments gives an exact optimum: an asset's) Tj T*
(second increment cannot precede its more valuable first increment. Integer budgets buy) Tj T*
(only whole increments; unused budget remains unspent. No record gets incompatible final) Tj T*
(actions. This proof depends on the stated equal costs, homogeneous effects and additive) Tj T*
(risk. It does not generalise to arbitrary engineering project costs or interacting) Tj T*
(failures. The implementation is frontier/capital.py; small exhaustive tests compare) Tj T*
(every feasible action combination.) Tj T*
